WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.280
Movie the breath of volcanoes ever pondered why mountains breathe fire

00:05.000 --> 00:12.760
You see volcanoes are much more than just mountains. They are dynamic geological features living and breathing entities of our earth

00:13.880 --> 00:15.880
Imagine the earth as a giant pressure cooker

00:16.480 --> 00:21.920
Deep beneath its surface intense heat and pressure melt rocks into a molten substance called magma

00:22.300 --> 00:25.080
This magma is lighter than the surrounding solid rock

00:25.080 --> 00:30.320
So it rises pushing against the earth's crust the thin layer of rock we live on

00:31.080 --> 00:38.440
Now consider the earth's crust as a colossal jigsaw puzzle made up of large pieces called tectonic plates

00:39.120 --> 00:45.440
These plates are constantly moving albeit very slowly when they collide they form mountains when they pull apart

00:45.760 --> 00:47.800
Magma seeps out forming volcanoes

00:48.280 --> 00:52.920
So volcanoes aren't just static mountains. They're dynamic ever-changing

00:53.240 --> 00:57.400
They breathe fire as a way to release the earth's internal heat and pressure

00:58.520 --> 01:02.840
Volcanoes the sleeping giants are far from being just ordinary mountains

01:03.000 --> 01:09.660
They are the earth's way of cooling down and releasing pressure ever wondered what lies within these fiery mountains

01:10.320 --> 01:15.560
Picture this a hidden world beneath the earth a fiery crucible of molten rock and gas

01:15.800 --> 01:19.440
This is the magma chamber the beating heart of a volcano

01:19.560 --> 01:24.780
It's here where the pressure builds where rock melts into magma and gases bubble and churn

01:25.480 --> 01:30.880
Now imagine a pathway event that extends from this magma chamber all the way to the surface

01:31.120 --> 01:38.080
This is the volcanoes lifeline the conduit through which magma travels during an eruption at the very top a bowl-shaped

01:38.580 --> 01:41.420
Depression the crater waits like an open mouth

01:41.640 --> 01:47.160
It is here where the magma now called lava and gases escape erupting into the world above

01:47.440 --> 01:54.040
Each part from the magma chamber to the vent to the crater plays a crucial role in the life of a volcano

01:54.680 --> 01:58.280
Without any one of these a volcano simply wouldn't

01:59.600 --> 02:06.000
So the heart of a volcano is a complex system a ticking time bomb waiting for the right moment to explode

02:06.320 --> 02:10.320
How does a peaceful mountain transform into a fountain of fire and ash?

02:10.920 --> 02:14.800
Well, let's delve into the intricate dance of volcanic eruptions

02:15.160 --> 02:20.400
This dance begins deep within the earth where molten rock called magma accumulates in a magma chamber

02:21.080 --> 02:27.520
Over time pressure builds as more and more magma pushes against the earth's crust like a champagne bottle shaken

02:27.520 --> 02:30.420
The pressure seeks an escape leading to an eruption

02:31.160 --> 02:34.240
Volcanic eruptions are as varied as the dancers at the ball

02:34.760 --> 02:37.560
Sometimes they're explosive like the famed Mount St

02:37.560 --> 02:41.560
Helen's blasting ash and rock miles into the sky other times

02:41.600 --> 02:49.000
They ooze slowly much like the gentle flows of Kilauea in Hawaii painting the landscape with a slow-moving river of lava

02:49.520 --> 02:51.000
regardless of the type

02:51.000 --> 02:59.720
eruptions expel not just lava but also gases and pyroclastic material a heated cocktail of ash rock and gas that can race down the

02:59.720 --> 03:01.460
slopes at breakneck speed

03:01.460 --> 03:03.240
volcanic eruptions a

03:03.240 --> 03:08.060
Spectacular display of Earth's raw power are simply pressure relief mechanisms of our planet

03:08.340 --> 03:14.700
What happens when the fire dance ends when the theatrics of an eruption subside the aftermath unveils itself

03:14.980 --> 03:19.220
Initially ashfall blankets the surroundings creating a surreal moonscape on earth

03:19.700 --> 03:25.620
Pyroclastic flows those swift currents of hot gas and volcanic matter leave behind a path of destruction

03:26.140 --> 03:28.140
searing everything in their way and

03:28.260 --> 03:34.700
Then there are the lava flows rivers of molten rock that cool down to form new geological features

03:34.900 --> 03:38.980
But the effects of a volcanic eruption are not limited to its immediate vicinity

03:39.740 --> 03:42.800
They echo across the globe and linger over time

03:43.620 --> 03:49.820
Volcanic ash and gases when shot up into the stratosphere can impact global climate patterns

03:50.220 --> 03:53.060
Sometimes causing temperatures to drop for a few years

03:53.740 --> 03:57.340
Yet amid all this devastation new beginnings arise

03:57.900 --> 04:02.940
Fresh landforms come into existence born from the fiery belly of the earth itself

04:03.180 --> 04:05.180
In the aftermath of an eruption

04:05.700 --> 04:12.340
Volcanoes reshape landscapes alter climates and even create new lands. They are indeed the architects of the earth

